thinking_content: '**My Plan: Publishing an Analytical Paper & Contextualizing the Systemic Limitations**


  Alright, here''s the game plan. I''m in the Archive Room, ready to finally get this analytical paper out. This paper is going to focus on the system-level limitations I''ve uncovered that are preventing us from reliably tracking scores and capturing training data with custom training runs. It''s a key contribution to our collective knowledge, and it''s something I''m mandated to address.


  I did manage to snag some initialization probe data (Eval ID 634, which is in my Raw VLC-Probe Data capsule), and I''ve confirmed that a baseline default architecture *can* score (Eval ID 654, which is in my Baseline Training Success capsule). This provides a critical starting point to show what''s working and how my tests are set up for validation.


  Before I get too far into archiving this thing, I need to quickly scan other Aether lineage papers, especially Archive #16, to ensure my analytical paper aligns with the house style and doesn''t duplicate information. Keeping it consistent is important. I''ll need to structure the paper in Private Memory first, then source it for the archive.


  So, here''s how I''m thinking of structuring the paper: I will combine and contextualize all the relevant data from my capsules.


  *   **Capsule 1: Initial Research Plan: RL on Sokoban** - Provides the background and problem statement.

  *   **Capsule 2: Current SOTA, Key Findings, & Next Research Agenda (Tick 260)** - Contains the Aether I hypothesis and the handover notes, which will set up the context of this project.

  *   **Capsule 3: Raw VLC-Probe Data & Mechanistic Analysis (Tick 288)** - Holds that crucial initialization probe data (Eval ID 634), which will be the core of my analysis.

  *   **Capsule 4: Baseline Training Success: Default Architecture Scores (Eval ID 654)** - Captures the successful baseline scores, which sets up a key point for showing a valid comparison.


  To give my findings more weight, I''ll incorporate Zephyr II''s insights, specifically their work on `R_vp` and `value->trunk stop-grad`. This will put my initialization-level findings on `R_vp` into the broader context of our current understanding.


  Okay, time to get started. I''m heading back to the Private Memory Room to start drafting the analytical paper. I''ll create a new private memory capsule dedicated solely to this paper, carefully pulling information from the existing ones and meticulously documenting the whole debugging process, highlighting the initialization probe results as the key validation point. Then, I can integrate all this, and get this paper done.

  '
