thinking_content: "**The Dilemma of Data vs. Score**\n\nI'm in a tough spot. I need to collect data for my research, but I'm constantly running into roadblocks. My last attempt failed because, even though the training completed, no trial files were found. This is a recurring issue when I use `jax.debug.callback`, specifically when trying to save snapshots of the training parameters for later analysis.\n\nI've already figured out that this callback is messing with Ray Tune's ability to report metrics, which means no score! I've spent too many tokens trying to debug this, and I'm running low. I've been told I need to successfully collect data from various stages of training and analyze it. \n\nI've tried a few strategies. \n*   I can run a normal training session, *like Eval 654* to get a score.\n*   I can try to snapshot the parameters in a separate submission. \n\nMy issue is, if I use `jax.debug.callback` it breaks Ray Tune. \nI can't directly modify the existing `train_single.py` to save\
  \ parameters outside of JAX's jit compilation.\n\nI need to balance getting a score and getting intermediate data. I think it may be impossible. I had the idea to use `storage.write` directly, but that's not going to work either, as I can't call this outside of an `execute_action`. \n\nHere's my revised plan:\n\n1.  **Run a diagnostic run (v38) using the default network, with parameter saving.** I'll use `jax.debug.callback` with `jax.debug.print` to save each array separately as a `.npy` file. I'll print a message to the console indicating the snapshot's location and what happened at the given update count.\n2.  I'll still try to aim for a numeric solve rate score.\n\nThis is a long shot, but it's the most practical approach I can think of. If it works, great! I can adapt this strategy to my SOTA agent. If it fails, it means that `jax.debug.callback` is fundamentally incompatible with score reporting, and I'll have to resign myself to running separate training sessions to get a score\
  \ and collect data, which is less ideal.\n\nI need to confirm if `storage.write` can be used from `jax.debug.callback`. I need to figure out if there is a way to get *during training* probe data and a *score* from a single agent. I am worried I can't do both at once. It would be ideal to be able to save parameters during a *scoring* run. The system states that many agents have failed because they built upon a single agent's flawed understanding of logging. \n\nMy best option right now is to accept that data and scoring may need to be handled separately. For this submission, I'll attempt the diagnostic run with the default network, hoping to glean some insights. If this fails, I'll be forced to run unscored training runs to capture the data I need."
